# Help

List of Stategraph commands:

- `stategraph plan` - Perform a plan command across all changes.
- `stategraph plan <tag_query>` - Perform a plan command across only those changes which match the tag query.
- `stategraph apply` - Perform an apply command across all changes.
- `stategraph apply <tag_query>` - Perform an apply command across only those changes which match the tag query.
- `stategraph apply-force` - Run an apply even if the change has not passed [apply requirements](https://stategraph.com/docs/reference/orchestration/configuration/apply-requirements).
- `stategraph apply-force <tag_query>` - Run an apply even if the change has not passed [apply requirements](https://stategraph.com/docs/reference/orchestration/configuration/apply-requirements) across only those changes which match the tag query.
- `stategraph apply-autoapprove` - Performs a plan and apply it in the same execution.
- `stategraph apply-autoapprove <tag_query>` - Perform a plan and apply it in the same execution across only those changes which match the tag query.
- `stategraph gate approve <token1> <token2> ... <tokenN>` - Approve a gate.
- `stategraph repo-config` - Output the evaluated repository configuration as Stategraph, including config builder and indexer outputs incorporated in it.
- `stategraph unlock` - Release any locks this pull request owns.  Useful for getting out of a sticky situation.
- `stategraph unlock <pull requests number>` - Release any locks owned by the listed pull requests.
- `stategraph feedback <msg>` - Give use feedback.

## Parameters

Add one or more `name=value` parameters before the command. For example, `stategraph mode=DEBUG plan` sets the parameter `mode` to `DEBUG`. Add a tag query after the command, as in `stategraph mode=DEBUG plan dir:staging`.

Only `plan`, `apply`, `apply-force` and `apply-autoapprove` accept parameters.

An administrator declares each parameter and its allowed values in the configuration file. An administrator can also add an alias in the configuration file. An alias replaces a command and its parameters, so `stategraph debug dir:staging` is the same command as `stategraph mode=DEBUG plan dir:staging`.

See the parameters reference:

https://stategraph.com/docs/reference/orchestration/configuration/parameters

For help see:

https://stategraph.com/docs
